<header><h2>why build it like this?</h2></header>
<div class="why">
  <div>
    <h3>gpu</h3>
    <p>spends silicon on <b>hiding latency for you</b>: caches, thousands of parked warps, a scheduler. so the model stays simple: launch lots of threads</p>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <h3>tenstorrent</h3>
    <p>spends it on <b>sram and matrix engines</b>, and leaves the scheduling to software. data flows tile to tile instead of round-tripping through memory</p>
  </div>
</div>
<ul class="why-l">
  <li class="step">for brighten, honestly, the gpu is the easier tool. this chip is built for big matrix math, where planning every byte pays off</li>
  <li class="step">the payoff: blackhole-py decodes llama 3 8b (fp8, short prompts) at <b>81%</b> of the card's 512 GB/s memory bandwidth, because every byte's path is planned</li>
  <li class="step">the cost: a missing wait gives a fast <i>wrong</i> answer, a buffer that can never be reserved hangs it. the compiler and libraries carry most people</li>
</ul>
